{
  // Cloudflare's config for this Worker (the "yo7" project connected via
  // Workers & Pages -> Connect to Git). Cloudflare itself runs no build
  // step here — it serves pure static assets exactly as committed, this
  // file's only real job is the "not_found_handling" setting below.
  // (There IS a build/minify step, but it runs in GitHub Actions before
  // Cloudflare ever sees the repo — see README.md and scripts/minify.py
  // — so from Cloudflare's own perspective this remains a static-assets
  // deploy with no build command of its own.)
  //
  // The 9 info pages and hundreds of product pages generated by
  // scripts/generate-static-pages.py / generate-product-pages.py are real
  // files at real paths (about/index.html etc.) and get served directly,
  // no special handling needed. But most of the app — /shop, /checkout,
  // /orders, /admin, search, any product not in the hardcoded 76 — only
  // ever existed as a client-side hash route (#/shop) with no matching
  // file on disk. single-page-application here is what makes a real,
  // fresh visit (or reload, or shared link) to one of those paths work:
  // instead of Cloudflare 404ing on a path with no matching file, it
  // serves this same index.html, and the app's own router (see
  // syncHashFromPath()/route() in index.html) figures out which view to
  // show from the URL itself, then renders it.
  //
  // "main" adds worker.js on top of that same static-assets deploy, only
  // for the one route (/cdn-img/*, a Supabase Storage image proxy) that
  // needs real logic - a request whose path matches a real file on disk
  // is still served directly by the assets layer without ever reaching
  // worker.js (see that file's own comment), so every static page/asset
  // above behaves exactly as it did before this was added. "binding"
  // names how worker.js reaches that same assets layer itself
  // (env.ASSETS.fetch()) for every other request, which is what keeps
  // the single-page-application fallback working now that something
  // other than the assets layer answers first.
  "name": "yo7",
  "compatibility_date": "2025-01-01",
  "main": "worker.js",
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "not_found_handling": "single-page-application"
  }
}
